Single-database configuration for Flask.

A rundown of the db and migrations folders:

In db, we are handling all aspects of the back-end. This means everything database-wise, so have your
database engineer read this file in its entirety.

You will need to learn Flask-SQLAlchemy in order to understand and make changes to the preexisting code.
Use this tutorial: https://www.digitalocean.com/community/tutorials/how-to-use-flask-sqlalchemy-to-interact-with-databases-in-a-flask-application#introduction

If confused about any of the code or files, ask Wuyou Zhan for explanation. If confused specifically about the connections to the website, ask Ishant Mekala or any of your team's juniors for advice.

So, the db folder is a very fragile place. We have had many errors with it, including 
duplicate html files and file deletion accidents. The best way to avoid such things is to not delete
ANYTHING in this folder. Truly, the only file you might even want to consider deleting is this one, and 
I advise against it, as this advice may prove to be useful. For your team and for future teams.
So, do not delete anything in the db folder, anything in the subfolders, and try not to delete essential 
parts of the app.py file.

Quick sidenote regarding .env - the .env file holds crucial information to connect you to the database.

The first URI connects you to your localhost (it may be the one currently commented out).
This is used for testing.
The second URI connects to the actual server, when you are ready to migrate your database version.
Read my advice in the app.py file for more information. And DO NOT use this for testing.

The .gitignore is used to ignore the .env file, as it should not be stored in the github repo.

The templates folder is essential for template rendering, update html as needed.
The static folder has styling and js.
The instance and pycache are autogenerated folders, do not touch.

Finally, the bulk - migrations.

Migrations are a way to easily manage the versions of your database and push changes to a server.
I have provided the essential commands that I have found in the app.py file, commented.
DO NOT DELETE ANY FILES IN THE MIGRATIONS FOLDER. Doing so will raise errors that 
take hours to fix (reinstalling everything), as we have personal experience with.

Abstraction is key here - you do not need to know exactly what they do, just know they are 
all essential. Versions stores the database versions.

This is my take on the database, as I was the main database engineer for the first generation 
1086 attendance tracker project team. We didn't finish it, but we sure as heck did our best.

Thank you for reading, and good luck, future database engineer. 

Wuyou Zhan, Sophomore, 04/16/2026

